      title: "The Revolution of 1800",
      body: `## A bitter campaign

The election of 1800 matched Adams against Jefferson again, with Charles Cotesworth Pinckney of South Carolina as the second Federalist candidate and **Aaron Burr** of New York as the second Republican. It was one of the most vicious campaigns in American history. Federalist newspapers and clergymen denounced Jefferson as an atheist and a Jacobin who would bring the French Revolution's bloodshed to America; one Federalist warned that under Jefferson "murder, robbery, rape, adultery, and incest will be openly taught and practiced." Republicans portrayed Adams as a monarchist who wanted to make his office hereditary. The journalist **James Callender**, secretly paid by Jefferson, wrote that Adams was a "hideous hermaphroditical character"; Callender was convicted under the Sedition Act and jailed. (Two years later, angry at Jefferson for not rewarding him, Callender would publish the charge that Jefferson had fathered children with an enslaved woman, Sally Hemings.)

## The tie

The Republicans won. But because each elector cast two votes, and the Republican electors all voted for both Jefferson and Burr, the two tied with 73 electoral votes each; Adams received 65 and Pinckney 64. Under the Constitution, the tie went to the House of Representatives, where each state delegation had one vote, and where the outgoing Federalist majority could decide between the two Republicans.

Many Federalists preferred Burr, an opportunist whom they thought they could manage, to Jefferson, whom they hated. Burr did not withdraw. For six days in February 1801, the House voted thirty-five times without producing a majority, while rumors spread that Republican militias in Pennsylvania and Virginia would march on the capital if Jefferson were denied. **Hamilton**, who loathed Jefferson's politics but considered Burr dangerous and unprincipled, lobbied Federalists to accept Jefferson. On the thirty-sixth ballot, on February 17, several Federalists abstained, and Jefferson was elected. To prevent a repetition, Congress proposed the **Twelfth Amendment**, ratified in 1804, which requires electors to cast separate ballots for president and vice president.

## A peaceful transfer of power

On March 4, 1801, Jefferson was inaugurated in the unfinished capital city of Washington, where Congress had moved only months before. Adams, bitter, left the city before dawn and did not attend. But he left. For the first time, power passed peacefully from one party to another in a national election—an outcome by no means certain in a world where revolutions and coups were common and where each party had considered the other illegitimate.

Jefferson's **first inaugural address** was conciliatory. "Every difference of opinion is not a difference of principle," he said. "We have called by different names brethren of the same principle. We are all Republicans, we are all Federalists." He promised "a wise and frugal Government, which shall restrain men from injuring one another, shall leave them otherwise free to regulate their own pursuits of industry and improvement." Years later, in 1819, he described his election as "the revolution of 1800...as real a revolution in the principles of our government as that of 1776 was in its form." Historians still debate how revolutionary it was.

## The three-fifths clause

Jefferson's victory had an uncomfortable foundation. Because the Constitution counted three-fifths of enslaved people in apportioning seats in the House and therefore electoral votes, the Southern states had more electoral votes than their free populations warranted. Without the extra electors created by the three-fifths clause, Adams would probably have won. Federalists bitterly called Jefferson the "Negro President," the title the historian Garry Wills adopted for his 2003 study of the slave power in the early republic.

## Gabriel's conspiracy

In the same year, in the same state, enslaved people made their own claim to the Revolution's principles. In the summer of 1800 an enslaved blacksmith named **Gabriel**, near Richmond, organized a plan to seize the city, take the governor, James Monroe, hostage, and negotiate for freedom. He reportedly planned to spare Quakers, Methodists, and Frenchmen, whom he regarded as friends of liberty, and his followers spoke the language of the Revolution and of the Haitian rising. A torrential storm delayed the attack, and two enslaved men revealed the plot. Gabriel and about twenty-five others were hanged. One of the condemned reportedly told the court that he had nothing more to offer in his defense than George Washington would have had if the British had captured him. Virginia responded by tightening its slave laws.

## The midnight judges

In its last weeks, the Federalist Congress passed the **Judiciary Act of 1801**, creating new federal judgeships, and Adams filled them with Federalists, signing commissions until late on his last night in office. These "midnight judges" outraged Republicans. Adams also appointed his secretary of state, **John Marshall** of Virginia, as chief justice. Marshall would serve for thirty-four years and, as you will see in Lesson II, make the Supreme Court a coequal branch of government. The Federalists had lost the presidency and Congress; they held the courts. The new Republican Congress repealed the Judiciary Act of 1801 in 1802, abolishing the new circuit judgeships, and Jefferson's administration refused to deliver several of the commissions that Adams had signed but not sent out. One of the disappointed appointees, a Maryland Federalist named William Marbury, sued.

## The end of the Federalists

The Federalists never again won the presidency. They remained strong in New England, but their opposition to the War of 1812 and the Hartford Convention destroyed them as a national party. Yet much of their program endured: the funded debt, the national bank (which Jefferson's party would recharter in 1816), a strong executive, a federal judiciary with the power to interpret the Constitution, and the precedent of a national government that could enforce its laws.

> **Pause and consider.** Jefferson called his election "the revolution of 1800." What, if anything, was revolutionary about it?`,
