      title: "Interpreting the Federalist Era",
      body: `## Hamilton and Jefferson

Few rivalries in American history have been argued over as long as that between Hamilton and Jefferson. For two centuries, Americans have used the two men as symbols of competing visions of the nation. In one version, Hamilton stands for a strong national government, commerce, industry, finance, and national power; Jefferson for limited government, agriculture, states' rights, and individual liberty. Which side Americans have taken has depended on their own politics.

In the nineteenth century, Jefferson was the hero of the Democratic Party, and Hamilton the hero of the Whigs and Republicans. In the early twentieth century, Progressive reformers such as **Herbert Croly**, in *The Promise of American Life* (1909), called for using "Hamiltonian means" (a strong national government) to achieve "Jeffersonian ends" (equality and democracy). Franklin Roosevelt claimed Jefferson as the champion of the common man and built a memorial to him in Washington. In recent decades, **Ron Chernow**'s biography *Alexander Hamilton* (2004) and the musical it inspired made Hamilton, the immigrant from the Caribbean, a hero of a new, multicultural America—an interpretation historians have criticized for overlooking Hamilton's elitism and his own entanglements with slavery, since he bought and sold enslaved people on behalf of his in-laws.

## Economic interpretations

In 1915 the Progressive historian **Charles Beard**, following his earlier study of the Constitution, published *Economic Origins of Jeffersonian Democracy*, which argued that the parties of the 1790s represented opposed economic interests: the Federalists, the "capitalistic" interests of financiers, merchants, and speculators; the Republicans, the agrarian interests of farmers and planters. Later historians found the picture more complicated. Many Republicans were merchants and speculators; many Federalists were farmers; and the parties divided as much along lines of region, religion, ethnicity, and attitudes toward Britain and France as along lines of economic interest.

## Ideological interpretations

As you saw in AMH 101, Bernard Bailyn and Gordon Wood emphasized the ideology of the Revolution: the fear of power and corruption inherited from English opposition writers. Historians such as **Lance Banning**, in *The Jeffersonian Persuasion* (1978), and **Drew McCoy**, in *The Elusive Republic* (1980), argued that the Republican opposition to Hamilton grew from this ideology. Jefferson and Madison saw in Hamilton's program a re-creation of the system of Sir Robert Walpole, whose use of the national debt, the Bank of England, and patronage to control Parliament had been denounced as the corruption of the English constitution. Their fear was genuine, and it shaped their politics.

Joyce Appleby, in *Capitalism and a New Social Order* (1984), challenged this view, arguing that the Republicans were not backward-looking agrarians but liberal believers in a free-market society of independent producers, opposed to Hamilton's use of government to favor a privileged few. The debate between "republican" and "liberal" interpretations of the early republic was one of the liveliest in American historiography in the 1980s.

## The Age of Federalism

**Stanley Elkins and Eric McKitrick**'s *The Age of Federalism* (1993), a massive study of the 1790s, tried to understand both sides on their own terms, and emphasized the extraordinary uncertainty of the decade. The leaders of the new republic had no precedents for a national republican government on such a scale. They did not know whether it would survive. Their fears, which seem exaggerated in hindsight, were reasonable in a world of revolutions, wars, and collapsing governments.

**Gordon Wood**'s *Empire of Liberty* (2009), the Oxford History volume on the years 1789–1815, argued that the Federalists were trying to create a stable, hierarchical republic led by gentlemen, and that the Republican victory of 1800 unleashed a democratic, commercial, individualistic society that neither Hamilton nor Jefferson had intended or would have entirely approved.

## Who was left out

Recent historians have asked who was excluded from the politics of the 1790s. The Naturalization Act of 1790 limited citizenship to "free white" people. Women could not vote, except briefly in New Jersey, where the state constitution of 1776 allowed "all inhabitants" who met a property qualification to vote, and some women and free Black men did so until the legislature restricted the vote to white men in 1807. Native nations were treated as foreign nations when it suited the United States and as dependents when it did not. And both parties, despite the antislavery sentiments of some of their leaders, protected slavery. Congress in 1793 passed a Fugitive Slave Act, signed by Washington, allowing slaveholders to seize alleged runaways in any state.

## A republic in the making

What, then, did the 1790s accomplish? The Constitution became a working government. The national government established its credit and its authority to enforce its laws. Washington established the precedents of the presidency, including the limit of two terms. The first parties emerged, and, for the first time, power passed peacefully from one to the other. These were not small things. The world of the 1790s was littered with failed republics. The American one survived.

> **Pause and consider.** Hamilton and Jefferson have been used as symbols by every generation of Americans. Which of their visions seems to you to have prevailed in the long run—and is the opposition between them a true one?`,
